===Alternative forms===
* {{sense|brick}} {{l|en|klinker}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɪŋkə(ɹ)/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɪŋkɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋkə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|clink|er}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|nl|klinkaerd}}, later {{m|nl|klinker}}, from {{m|nl|klinken||to ring, resound}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A very hard [[brick]] used for [[paving]] customarily made in the [[Netherlands]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938|author=w:Xavier Herbert|title=[[w:Capricornia (novel)|Capricornia]]|location=New York|publisher=D. Appleton-Century|year_published=1943|chapter=XXXII|page=581|url=https://archive.org/details/capricornianovel00herb
|passage=She left the road at the little shed where he whom she still regarded as her father used to keep his tricycle, and walked up the '''clinker''' path towards the house.}}
# A mass of bricks fused together by intense heat. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# [[slag|Slag]] or [[ash]] produced by intense heat in a [[furnace]], [[kiln]] or [[boiler]] that forms a hard residue upon cooling. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1942|month=July-August|author=Philip Spencer|title=On the footplate in Egypt|journal=Railway Magazine|page=208|text=The coal was terrible stuff—Indian, Abdul told me. The "dart" was used often and I saw some monster '''clinkers'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1944|author=w:Emily Carr|title=w:The House of All Sorts|chapter=Dew and Alarm Clocks|pageurl=http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks01/0100121h.html
|passage=Cold and grim sat that malevolent brute the furnace, greedy, bottomless—its grate bars clenched over '''clinkers''' which no shaker could dislodge.}}
# An [[intermediate]] [[product]] in the manufacture of [[Portland cement]], obtained by [[sinter|sintering]] limestone and alumino-silicate materials such as clay into [[nodule|nodules]] in a cement kiln.
# Hardened [[volcanic]] [[lava]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=This wall of rock, which had no doubt once formed the lip of the crater, was about a mile and a half thick, and still covered with '''clinker'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=w:Richard Fortey|title=The Earth|page=10|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2011
|passage=Nobody could pretend that a huge slope of '''clinker''' is aesthetically pleasing.}}
# A [[scum]] of [[oxide]] of [[iron]] formed in [[forging]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}

